    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/providencecollege/jobs/5519776/assistant-director-of-financial-aid": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Financial Aid",
      "description": "Assistant Director of Financial Aid Salary $65,300.00 - $68,000.00 Annually Location Providence College Job Type Full-time Staff Job Number PC00354 Division Executive Vice President Department Financial Aid Opening Date 10/19/2026 FLSA Exempt Bargaining Unit NU Description Benefits Conducted under the auspices of the Dominican Friars, Providence College is a Catholic, Dominican, liberal arts institution of higher education and a community committed to academic excellence in pursuit of the truth, growth in virtue, and service of God and neighbor. We seek candidates who can affirm and contribute to our mission. Providence College is also an equal opportunity employer and is committed to fostering a Beloved Community — an environment that reflects the rich diversity of the human family and is marked by justice, charity and the promotion of the common good and flourishing of all. We invite applications from qualified individuals who possess the experience, knowledge and commitment to develop the diverse learning environment envisioned as part of the Beloved Community. Women and minority candidates are strongly encouraged to apply. The College’s Non-Discrimination policy can be found Notice of Non-Discrimination (PDF document) Overview In addition to core counseling and compliance responsibilities, serve as the functional lead for two major program areas, Marketing, Communications & Outreach, and Athletics Program Coordinator. Duties and Responsibilities Conduct comprehensive review of financial aid files, including complex verification and professional judgment appeals for federal and institutional aid programs. Evaluate financial documentation, resolve conflicting information, and ensure adherence to federal, state, and institutional regulatory requirements. Oversee the packaging, revision, and finalization of financial aid awards across all aid programs. Coordinate closely with Student Accounts/Bursar to ensure accurate disbursement, reconciliation, adjustments, and compliance with applicable regulations and institutional policies. Provide advanced, individualized advising to students and families regarding financial aid eligibility, financing strategies, and aid processes, particularly in complex or high-impact situations. (40%) Direct office-wide communications, overseeing the delivery of all outgoing messages to prevent information saturation and ensure a consistent tone and brand. Oversee the management of all website content and consumer information, establishing a system for regular updates to information, forms, and educational literature while maintaining WordPress and Dubot training and backups. Develop and execute digital marketing campaigns across social media (Facebook, Instagram, etc.), researching peer trends and emerging platforms to reach students and parents through their preferred electronic channels. Lead the design and preparation of all departmental events, webinars, presentations, and instructional materials, creating robust, accessible ways to share financial aid information for virtual, classroom, and other in-person spaces. Coordinate the delivery of aid messaging through diverse campus platforms—including Campus Communicator’s Virtual Advisor, Slate, Morning Mail, TV displays, and satellite locations—to ensure information reaches students both inside and outside the classroom. Serve as the primary liaison to the Parent Program, development monthly newsletter content that helps families navigate the financial aid process and determine affordability. Manage the creation of inclusive and accessible resources, including multi-language materials, and ensure all web content meets Section 504. (30%) Direct the administration of approximately $13.6 million in athletic-based scholarships, serving as the primary liaison to the Bursar, Office of Admission, and the Athletics Compliance Officer for more than 200 student-athletes across 15 teams. Direct the Early Athletic Read process for 60+ recruits annually, overseeing communications via Slate and the Pcathleticreads@providence.edu inbox and performing manual financial aid calculations and document analysis outside of standard system packaging to support early aid estimates and offers. Partner with the Athletic Compliance Officer to oversee National Letter of Intent (NLI) administration and athletic award posting, verifying award levels by sport, capturing revisions resulting from billing adjustments, team separations, or withdrawals, and ensuring all signed agreements are properly imaged and awards are accurately confirmed in Banner prior to each packing cycle. Coordinate with the Bursar’s Office to ensure accuracy and billing integrity, proactively monitoring non-disbursed aid and maintain shared systems to support transparency and stakeholder access. Interpret NCAA regulations to ensure ongoing compliance, annually updating programmatic instructions, methodology tables, award/email templates, and annual office documentation, and providing training and guidance to peers. Lead data collection and documentation for the annual A-133 financial aid audit. (20%) Lead annual planning with the card office to review and update program policies and the web nomination form, ensuring the process remains effective for the upcoming academic year. Manage the allocation of the “Meal Swipe Bank” determining distribution levels to support as many students as possible while staying within semester limits. Oversee the review of all web nominations submitted by students, faculty, or staff, ensuring that all requests are handled with strict confidentiality and within a 24–48-hour window. Evaluate eligibility for a diverse student population, including commuter and international students by verifying financial need and current meal plan status. Maintain professional communication standards for the program, providing approved students with clear, updated instructions on how to redeem swipes at Raymond Dining Hall. Coordinate with the card office and external partners to finalize meal uploads and manage the primary tracking system to ensure the program remains within its institutional budget. (5%) Participate in and support outreach events, orientations, workshops, and enrollment-related programming in collaboration with campus partners. Provide training and cross-training to financial aid staff to support operational continuity, consistency of practice, and knowledge sharing across the office. Contribute to office-wide initiatives, operational planning, and process improvement efforts that enhance service delivery, efficiency, and compliance. Serve on institutional committees and working groups as assigned, representing the office and providing subject matter expertise. (5%) MARGINAL DUTIES: Perform all other duties as may be required. Education and Experience Bachelor's degree from an accredited institution required. Three to five years’ experience in a financial aid environment or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Comprehensive knowledge of federal, state, and institutional financial aid regulations. Familiarity and ability to interpret Title IV legislation and “Dear Colleague Letters” to assist leadership in office compliance. Ability to maintain multiple priorities and meet deadlines in a fast-paced environment. Strong collaboration skills and the ability to work effectively with other departments. High degree of accuracy and attention to detail. Ability to exercise independent judgement and maintain confidentiality. Exceptional communication and interpersonal skills. Some nights and weekends as well as travel required. Supplemental Information PHYSICAL DEMANDS: The physical demands described here are representative of those that must be met by an employee to successfully perform the essential functions of this position. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. Sitting in a normal seated position for extended periods of time Reaching by extending hand(s) or arm(s) in any direction Finger dexterity required to manipulate objects with fingers rather than with whole hand(s) or arm(s), for example, using a keyboard Communication skills using the spoken word Ability to see within normal parameters Ability to hear within normal range Ability to move about DISCLAIMER: This job description indicates the general nature and level of work expected of the incumbent. It is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties, or responsibilities required of the incumbent. Incumbent may be asked to perform other duties as required. NOTICE OF E-VERIFY PARTICIPATION Providence College participates in E-Verify to confirm the identity and employment authorization of individuals hired to work in the United States. Before submitting this application, please review the following documents: E-Verify Notice of Participation Right to Work By continuing with this application, you acknowledge that you have been informed of our participation in E-Verify and have had the opportunity to review these notices. Providence College offers a comprehensive range of valuable benefits to eligible employees, which are designed to: Promote the health and well-being of you and your family Provide financial security while you are working Build financial resources for retirement Help you balance personal and work responsibilities Provide educational opportunities for you and your family These benefits include but are not limited to health, dental, 403b retirement plan, flexible benefits plan, tuition benefits, employee assistance plan, college paid basic life insurance and long-term disability insurance, and paid time off. To learn more, please visit our Employee Benefits website by clicking the following link: https://human-resources.providence.edu/benefits/ Employer Providence College (RI) Address 1 Cunningham Square Providence, Rhode Island, 02918-0001 Phone (401) 865-2341 Website www.providence.edu Apply"
